  "strategy": "This approach uses a minimal code structure with offline optimizations to save validation time and improve performance. The key stages are:\n1. Define global variable length\n2. Use toroidal tournament selection across candidate dimensions with dynamic boundaries\n3. Maintain inhomogeneous normalization throughout optimization phases \n4. Implement a hybrid crossover that reduces and redistributes convolution peaks\n5. Use exponential mutation scaling to control the distribution of variations \n6. Cache intermediate results to reduce redundant calculations \n7. Monitor time consumption for each validation step\n8. Final acceptance uses a multi-dimensional scaling rationale\n\nInitialization:\n- If 'height_sequence_1' is not None:\n   a = np.clip(height_sequence_1.astype(float), 0, 1000) \n   score_base = evaluate_sequence(a.tolist())\n   n_val = len(a)\nElse:\n   budget_seconds = budget_s  # Lower this to save time\n   n_val = 256  # Main candidate length\n   a = np.log(np.clip(rng.normal(0, 1, n_val) + 1, 0.01, None)) + 0.1\n   a = a * (np.clip(rng.exponential(scale=5, size=1)[0], 0.1, None))\n\nFitness evaluation for all candidates in the online trajectory:\n\ndef toroidal_fitness(candidate, n_range):  # candidates are normalized\n  score = evaluate_sequence(candidate.tolist())\n  if score == np.inf: return score\n  new_score = (2 * n_range[0] * np.max(score_candidate) / (score_base if candidate[0] == n_range[0] else score_sum[0]**2)\n  return new_score * candidate[-1]  # Global scaling\n\nGlobal optimization loop:\n\ngeneration = 0\npopulation_size = 200\ntournament_size = 10\nmutation_probability = 0.3\ncrossover_threshold = 0.5\nelite_count = 30\ncache_threshold = 10000\n\nwhile generation < 500 and time.monotonic() < deadline:\n  generation += 1\n  # Create distributed population\n  new_population = [make_structured_init(n_val, ' ') for i in range(population_size)]\n  new_population = standardize_population(new_population)\n  \n  # Evaluating on